---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { getIndex, getLatest, int } from '../lib/archive';

const index = getIndex();
const latest = getLatest();
---

<Base title="About" description="What this is, and what it is not.">
  <h1>About</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    Pitfield St publishes computed derivatives data and the code that
    produced it. Every trading day the pipeline ingests option chains, extracts
    forwards from put–call parity, fits arbitrage-free volatility surfaces,
    computes a full metric set, and publishes the results alongside the quality
    report describing what it had to throw away.
  </p>

  <h2>What it is not</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Not a signals service. There are no buy or sell recommendations, no alerts, no screeners.</li>
    <li>Not a claim of predictive edge. Nothing here is presented as tradeable.</li>
    <li>Not investment advice, and not a recommendation about any security.</li>
    <li>Not a real-time feed. Published quotes are end-of-day or delayed, never streaming.</li>
    <li>There is not a single backtest equity curve in the repository, and there never will be.</li>
  </ul>

  {latest?.synthetic && (
    <>
      <h2>On the current data</h2>
      <div class="banner" role="note">
        <strong>The archive currently runs on synthetic chains.</strong>
        <p>
          The pipeline, the calibration, the arbitrage checks and the statistics
          are real, and are exactly the code that will run against a licensed
          feed. The quotes they are presently run against are generated by a
          simulator in this repository, which prices chains from a known forward
          and known SVI parameters — that is what makes the calibration testable,
          since the test suite can assert the pipeline recovers the parameters it
          was handed.
        </p>
        <p>
          Nothing on this site describes an actual security today. Every page
          built on generated numbers says so at the top. When a licensed source
          is connected, the synthetic banner disappears and the archive begins
          accruing real observations — the code does not change.
        </p>
      </div>
    </>
  )}

  <h2>Data licensing</h2>
  <p>
    Market data will be sourced under a single-person, non-professional,
    non-redistributing licence. Published quotes are therefore end-of-day or
    delayed. Derived values — implied volatilities, greeks, SVI parameters,
    aggregates — are this project's own computations and are published freely.
  </p>

  <h2>Reproducibility</h2>
  <p>
    The repository is public and the scheduled runs are public with it, which
    means the run logs are third-party evidence that the pipeline has been
    operating unattended rather than a claim on this page. A clone plus
    <span class="num">make demo</span> reproduces a full day's output from raw
    inputs, including this site.
  </p>
  {index && (
    <table class="facts">
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>Days archived</th><td class="n">{int(index.n_days)}</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Contract-days</th><td class="n">{int(index.contract_days)}</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Latest session</th><td class="n">{index.latest_date}</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Pipeline version</th><td class="n">{index.pipeline_version}</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  )}

  <h2>Corrections</h2>
  <p>
    Nothing in the archive is ever overwritten. A correction is written as a new
    row with a later snapshot timestamp, and readers default to the first
    observation of a given contract on a given day. Rows carry the version of the
    code that wrote them, so output from a bug can be identified without deleting
    the record that it happened.
  </p>
</Base>

<style>
  .facts { max-width: 34rem; margin: 1.2rem 0; }
</style>
